{#
  T-6.01 — the order status, as text and colour.

  Usage:
    {% include 'components.orders.status' with { status: order.status } only %}

  Parameters:
    status  required — the platform's own status object: `name`, `color`,
            optionally `icon`.

  THE COLOUR IS THE PLATFORM'S, AND THERE IS NO STATUS→COLOUR MAP HERE ON
  PURPOSE. The artboards draw six pills — «تم التوصيل» green, «تم الشحن» and
  «بانتظار الدفع» amber, «فشل الدفع» red, «قيد التجهيز», «تم الإلغاء» and
  «مسترجعة» grey — and the obvious implementation is a lookup keyed on the
  status. It would be wrong: **a merchant can rename a status and add custom ones
  in the dashboard**, and a theme-side map would paint every status it had never
  heard of in the default colour, silently. `status.color` is the value the
  merchant themself set, so it is the one used.

  ONE INLINE DECLARATION, AND THE REST DERIVED FROM IT. The pill needs three
  colours — text, fill and edge — and only one arrives as data. The other two are
  `color-mix()` against `currentColor` in the stylesheet, so the whole pill turns
  on the single platform value and no second value is invented for it. This is
  the one place in the theme a colour is written inline, and it is dynamic
  content rather than a literal, which is the distinction the no-raw-hex rule
  draws. Upstream's own orders list did the same thing on the same field.

  COLOUR IS NEVER ALONE. `status.name` is the text of the pill, so the state is
  legible with no colour perceived at all — the criterion asks for text *plus*
  colour, and the text is what carries it.
#}
<span class="order-status" style="color: {{ status.color }}">
    {% if status.icon is defined and status.icon is not empty %}
        <i class="{{ status.icon }}" aria-hidden="true"></i>
    {% endif %}
    <span>{{ status.name }}</span>
</span>
